WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.673 --> 00:00:01.506
(click)

00:00:01.506 --> 00:00:02.370
- These are some of the storage units

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:04.920
where Russia houses its atomic warheads.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:07.230
Satellite images are a
key tool for the world

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:08.970
to keep an eye on the nuclear sites

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:10.593
dotted across the country.

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:13.170
As Russian President Vladimir Putin

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.630
suffers losses in
Ukraine, he has suggested

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.900
that he was ready to
use his nuclear arsenal.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.983
- (speaking Russian)

00:00:25.871 --> 00:00:27.840
- A nuclear strike is still widely seen

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.610
as an unlikely scenario,

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:31.770
but Western officials have
said they are concerned,

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:33.810
and President Biden warned
the world could face

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:35.670
the prospect of an Armageddon.

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:38.280
So how would a Russian
nuclear strike play out?

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.450
- It's not like there is a big red button

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:43.380
on the president's desk.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.990
- By looking at a map of
Russia, we can learn a lot

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:48.483
about how Putin would
activate his atomic force.

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:52.350
These dots represent the known locations

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:55.350
where Russia houses some
of its nuclear weapons.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:57.600
Bases like these have been
identified by analysts

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.310
who monitor nuclear arsenals

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:02.490
using satellite images and other sources.

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:03.840
The red ones indicate sites

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.390
where tactical nuclear
warheads are stored.

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.730
Typically, these are
lower yield weapons used

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:12.720
on delivery systems with
a range under 310 miles.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:13.890
And the blue dots represent

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:16.920
where some of Moscow's
strategic weapons are deployed.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:18.060
These have more power

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:21.330
and can travel up to about 9,000 miles.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:23.700
If Moscow decided to
launch a nuclear attack,

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.950
many Western and Ukrainian
officials believe

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:28.290
it would turn to tactical weapons.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.927
- Normally, it is assumed
that strategic weapons

00:01:30.927 --> 00:01:33.210
are the last things you touch.

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:34.770
- Hans Kristensen is the director

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:36.720
of the Nuclear Information Project,

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.690
and has been studying Russian
nuclear weapons for decades.

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:41.730
- The assumption is that
tactical nuclear weapons,

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:44.700
to the extent that they would
escalate to nuclear use,

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.190
would be the first that would be used.

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:50.730
- Russia has 2,889 warheads in storage,

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:52.770
according to American analysts.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:55.173
That's almost 1,000 more than the US.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.050
And even those tactical weapons

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:00.510
can be very powerful and destructive.

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:04.350
The RN-40 gravity bomb,
carried in jets such as these,

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:08.520
has the same estimated power
as 30 kilotons of dynamite.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.140
That's more than the
bombs that were dropped

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:14.640
on Hiroshima and Nagasaki,
at about 15 to 20 kilotons,

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.190
and much less than
Russia's strategic weapons.

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:20.190
These intercontinental
ballistic missiles, for example,

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:23.490
can have a yield of 300 to 800 kilotons.

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:25.740
A strike using those
weapons would be enough

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:28.863
to destroy any major city,
according to Kristensen.

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:31.710
If Russia was to actually deploy

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:34.110
tactical nuclear weapons at these sites,

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:37.350
it would first have to tackle
some logistical challenges.

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:39.120
That starts with retrieving the warheads

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.120
from the storage bunkers or
units that they're housed in.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:46.110
- Those weapons are all in
valid storage facilities.

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:49.110
- Pavel Podvig is a Geneva-based
nuclear weapons expert

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:51.000
who tracks Russian storage bases.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.640
- These are normally some distance

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.590
from the delivery systems.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:57.030
- Take this site in Belgorod

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:59.250
near the border with Ukraine, for example.

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:01.680
On a nuclear order, the
site personnel would have

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.080
to retrieve the warheads from bunkers.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:05.640
Then they would load them onto trucks

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:08.130
in special climate-controlled containers

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:09.780
and transport them to an airfield

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:12.903
or designated point near a
missile base like this one.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:16.650
Once they arrive, they would
then need to be installed

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:18.600
on a launcher or airplane.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:20.670
So actually deploying them for combat

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:23.010
could take many hours or up to a few days,

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:24.810
military analysts say.

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:26.910
That whole process would
be very hard to complete

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:28.770
without other countries realizing,

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:31.200
as movements are being
tracked by intelligence teams,

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.300
according to defense experts.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.540
So Russia appears to have
taken steps to be less visible.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:37.920
- If you look at the satellite photos

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:39.900
over these nuclear weapons storage sites,

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:43.260
they typically have sort of a roof

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:46.050
or a garage at the entrance.

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:49.440
So, any truck that drives
up goes into this garage,

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:52.320
so you can't see what
they're loading on board.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:53.550
- But increasing the chances

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:55.080
the message of a strike would spread

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:57.693
is the multiple personnel
involved along the way.

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:01.710
The group responsible for
Russia's nuclear weapons

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:04.710
is a special unit called
the 12th Chief Directorate.

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:07.110
Analysts say the individuals in that force

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:09.570
could potentially oppose strikes.

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:13.020
- The military would be
part of that process.

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:16.440
They would have a chance at least

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:20.430
to offer their opinion on the plan,

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:23.220
whether it would go ahead.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:25.530
- If movement at the
facilities was detected,

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:27.870
the West could respond
in a number of ways,

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:30.150
according to military analysts.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:32.250
One scenario is that
countries could target

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:35.670
Moscow's storage bases,
which carries huge risks.

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:37.863
while another is imposing more sanctions.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:41.220
Strategic nuclear weapons

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:43.650
like this intercontinental
ballistic missile

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:45.780
are even less likely to be used,

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:49.260
but hypothetically they would
be much quicker to deploy.

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:51.990
- They're standing in
their silos, the missiles,

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:54.840
or they're on board ballistic
missiles submarines,

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:57.300
and they're loaded with nuclear warheads,

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:59.883
ready to fly on, you
know, in a few minutes.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:03.390
- However, before any of that,

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:06.000
Putin would need to
actually order a strike,

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.070
and there's a series of
steps that need to take place

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:10.410
within the Kremlin for that to happen.

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:12.930
The process is secretive
and hard to monitor,

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:16.620
and could even have changed
since the Ukraine War began.

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:18.780
Defense analysts have
made an educated guess

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:22.680
how it would unfold, based
on documents and sources.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:27.300
- The theory is that
there are three people

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:28.810
that need to be involved

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:33.778
in an actual launch of a
Russian nuclear weapon,

00:05:33.778 --> 00:05:37.470
the Russian President, Putin,
and the Minister of Defense,

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:39.540
and then also the Chief
of the Armed Forces.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:42.450
They're supposed to have
three briefcases, so to speak,

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:45.510
electronic communication
devices they can use

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:47.463
to activate the nuclear option.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:50.760
- Some defense experts
believe that two out of three

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:52.440
of those officials would need to agree

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:53.820
for the strike to be carried out.

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:55.983
- If that is, in fact, the setup,

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:00.330
then it's very different
from the American setup

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:03.270
where the president has the
sole authority to launch this,

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:05.400
and he doesn't require...

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:07.950
he doesn't need approval from anyone else.

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:09.660
- In addition to the chain of command,

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:11.820
Russia has a strategic planning document,

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:13.260
or military doctrine,

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:16.890
which lays out how nuclear
weapons could be used.

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:19.500
The document says it allows
the use of nuclear weapons

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:22.650
if they, or other types of
weapons of mass destruction,

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:24.120
are used against it,

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:25.800
as well as in the event of aggression

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:28.650
that threatens the existence
of the Russian Federation.

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:30.300
The interpretation of that document

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:31.980
rests on the shoulders of Putin,

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:34.680
who has already said the
territorial integrity of Russia

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:35.940
may be threatened,

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:37.440
and has claimed the annexation

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:39.540
of four Ukrainian territories.

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:42.450
When asked if Russia could use
nuclear weapons in Ukraine,

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:44.250
Kremlin spokesman, Dmitry Peskov,

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:45.427
told the Wall Street Journal,

00:06:45.427 --> 00:06:49.047
"We consider such inflated
nuclear rhetoric unacceptable."

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:54.000
But with Moscow facing
setbacks in Ukraine,

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:55.860
officials and military analysts say

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:59.328
the use of tactical weapons
can't be completely ruled out.

00:06:59.328 --> 00:07:01.911
(upbeat music)

